<!--
  AdGuard Home decoders (jt-adguard 1.4)

  1. Query events, program name "adguard"

  Wire format (syslog, one JSON object per message, keys in any order):
    2026-10-11T02:43:17+00:00 dns-host adguard: {"Elapsed":215966,
    "IP":"192.0.2.171","QH":"dns.google","QT":"A","Upstream":null,
    "action":"Blocked","filterlist_id":1720886277,
    "filterlist_name":"HaGeZi's Encrypted DNS/VPN/TOR/Proxy Bypass",
    "filtertext":"||dns.google^"}

  AdGuard Home's own querylog.json does not carry the list NAME (only a
  numeric FilterListID), so this shape comes from a log shipper that reads
  querylog.json, maps the ID to the name from AdGuardHome.yaml and sends one
  JSON object per syslog message (the manifest's setup guide lists the fields).

  Field meanings:
    IP               the CLIENT that asked. A recursive resolver or firewall only
                     sees its own address as the source, so this is the only place
                     the real requester is recorded.
    QH / QT          queried host and query type
    action           what AdGuard did: "Blocked" or "Allowed"
    filterlist_id    AdGuard list ID; 0 custom rules, -1 hosts file, -2 blocked
                     services, -3 parental control, -4 safe browsing, -5 safe search
    filterlist_name  which blocklist matched
    filtertext       the matching rule text
    Elapsed          resolution time in nanoseconds
    Upstream         upstream resolver used, null when the answer was filtered

  Decoded fields for other rules and CDB lookups (all verified with logtest):
    srcip       = IP  (static field: address_match_key lookups, same_source_ip)
    dns.query   = QH  (dynamic field: match_key lookups against a domain list)
    data        = QH  (static field, only because first-time-seen tracking can
                       key on static fields alone; see below)
    every JSON key also stays available under its own name (QH, IP, filterlist_name ...).

  First time seen (fts): the pair (client address, queried domain) is the key,
  used by the rules that report the first lookup of a threat or bypass domain by
  a client. Wazuh 4.14 accepts only static fields in <fts> ("Wrong field" for a
  dynamic one), refuses fts on a decoder that has siblings of the same name, and
  refuses it on a decoder without <regex>. Hence the fts sits on the parent with
  a regex of its own, and the siblings decode the domain into "data" again,
  because a parent's regex fields do not survive when the children run.

  The program name is anchored (^adguard$). Unanchored it also matched
  "AdGuardHome", the program name of the service log, which then decoded as a
  query event with no fields.

  2. AdGuard Home service log (journald, syslog or a log file)

    2026/10/11 11:15:16.123456 [error] webapi: http error host=192.0.2.3:3000
      method=POST url=/control/login status=403 ip=192.0.2.50
      err="invalid username or password"
    2026/10/11 11:15:20.000001 [info] webapi: successful login user=admin ip=192.0.2.50

  Older releases (before the structured logger) write the same events as
    [error] POST 192.0.2.3:3000 /control/login: from ip 192.0.2.50: invalid username or password
    [info] auth: user "admin" successfully logged in from ip 192.0.2.50
  and both shapes are decoded, with a syslog header (decoder adguard-home,
  program AdGuardHome: journald, which is where a systemd install logs) or
  without one (decoder adguard-home-log: a log file read directly). The
  microseconds keep it apart from the nginx error log, whose built-in decoder
  expects "[" right after the seconds. An address written ::ffff:192.0.2.1 is
  stored as 192.0.2.1, so the private-range checks of the rules apply. The
  address is also kept in adguard.ip, a dynamic field, because rules cannot
  test srcip for ::1: any spelling of ::1 in <srcip> also matches every IPv4
  address (4.14.7, measured).

  1.4: API errors written by aghhttp.ErrorAndLog (every handler except the
  login) carry the client as raddr=IP:port and the path as request_uri=, not
  ip= and url= (v0.107.79 source, internal/aghhttp/aghhttp.go), for example
    2026/10/11 11:15:16.123456 [info] filtering: warning: http error
      host=192.0.2.3:3000 method=POST raddr=192.0.2.50:51234
      request_uri=/control/filtering/add_url status=400
      err="checking filter: path \"/etc/shadow\" does not match safe patterns"
  Until 1.3 such lines had no srcip and no url. The port is dropped, and an
  IPv6 peer is written [2001:db8::5]:51234. Also decoded since 1.4:
  adguard.path (the refused filter path), adguard.version (from the start
  line), adguard.filter_id ("deleted filter id=") and adguard.addr (pprof).
-->

<decoder name="adguard">
  <program_name>^adguard$</program_name>
  <regex type="pcre2">"QH":"([^"]+)"</regex>
  <order>data</order>
  <fts>name, srcip, data</fts>
</decoder>

<decoder name="adguard-json">
  <parent>adguard</parent>
  <use_own_name>yes</use_own_name>
  <plugin_decoder>JSON_Decoder</plugin_decoder>
</decoder>

<decoder name="adguard-json">
  <parent>adguard</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">(?i)"IP":"(?:::ffff:)?([^"]+)"</regex>
  <order>srcip</order>
</decoder>

<decoder name="adguard-json">
  <parent>adguard</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">^(?=.*?"QH":"([^"]+)")(?=.*?"QH":"([^"]+)")</regex>
  <order>dns.query, data</order>
</decoder>


<!-- The service log needs two parents: with a program name (journald, syslog)
     only decoders that name a program are tried, without one (a log file read
     directly) only decoders that do not. They need different names because
     each carries fts (user, address: first login of a user from an address),
     and fts is refused on a decoder whose name is used twice. The fts parent
     needs a regex of its own; the children decode the fields again. -->
<decoder name="adguard-home">
  <program_name>^AdGuardHome$</program_name>
  <prematch type="pcre2">^\d{4}/\d\d/\d\d \d\d:\d\d:\d\d\.\d+ (?:\d+#\d+ )?\[(?:debug|info|error|fatal|panic)\] </prematch>
  <regex type="pcre2">\[(\w+)\] </regex>
  <order>adguard.level</order>
  <fts>name, user, srcip</fts>
</decoder>

<decoder name="adguard-home-fields">
  <parent>adguard-home</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">\[(\w+)\] (?:(\w+): )?</regex>
  <order>adguard.level, adguard.module</order>
</decoder>

<decoder name="adguard-home-fields">
  <parent>adguard-home</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">(?i)^(?=.*? (?|(?:from ip |ip=)(?:::ffff:)?([0-9a-f:.]*[0-9a-f])|raddr=\[?(?:::ffff:)?([0-9a-f:.]*?[0-9a-f])\]?:\d+(?=\s|$)))(?=.*? (?|(?:from ip |ip=)(?:::ffff:)?([0-9a-f:.]*[0-9a-f])|raddr=\[?(?:::ffff:)?([0-9a-f:.]*?[0-9a-f])\]?:\d+(?=\s|$)))</regex>
  <order>srcip, adguard.ip</order>
</decoder>

<decoder name="adguard-home-fields">
  <parent>adguard-home</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">(?:url=|uri=| )(/control/[^\s:?"]+)</regex>
  <order>url</order>
</decoder>

<decoder name="adguard-home-fields">
  <parent>adguard-home</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2"> user(?|=([^\s"]+)|="([^"]*)"| "([^"]*)")</regex>
  <order>dstuser</order>
</decoder>

<decoder name="adguard-home-fields">
  <parent>adguard-home</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2"> status=(\d+)</regex>
  <order>adguard.status</order>
</decoder>

<decoder name="adguard-home-fields">
  <parent>adguard-home</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2"> path \\?"([^"\\]+)\\?" does not match safe patterns</regex>
  <order>adguard.path</order>
</decoder>

<decoder name="adguard-home-fields">
  <parent>adguard-home</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">AdGuard Home, version (v?\d[^\s",]*)</regex>
  <order>adguard.version</order>
</decoder>

<decoder name="adguard-home-fields">
  <parent>adguard-home</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">deleted filter id=(-?\d+)</regex>
  <order>adguard.filter_id</order>
</decoder>

<decoder name="adguard-home-fields">
  <parent>adguard-home</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">pprof: listening addr=(\S+)</regex>
  <order>adguard.addr</order>
</decoder>


<decoder name="adguard-home-log">
  <prematch type="pcre2">^\d{4}/\d\d/\d\d \d\d:\d\d:\d\d\.\d+ (?:\d+#\d+ )?\[(?:debug|info|error|fatal|panic)\] </prematch>
  <regex type="pcre2">\[(\w+)\] </regex>
  <order>adguard.level</order>
  <fts>name, user, srcip</fts>
</decoder>

<decoder name="adguard-home-log-fields">
  <parent>adguard-home-log</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">\[(\w+)\] (?:(\w+): )?</regex>
  <order>adguard.level, adguard.module</order>
</decoder>

<decoder name="adguard-home-log-fields">
  <parent>adguard-home-log</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">(?i)^(?=.*? (?|(?:from ip |ip=)(?:::ffff:)?([0-9a-f:.]*[0-9a-f])|raddr=\[?(?:::ffff:)?([0-9a-f:.]*?[0-9a-f])\]?:\d+(?=\s|$)))(?=.*? (?|(?:from ip |ip=)(?:::ffff:)?([0-9a-f:.]*[0-9a-f])|raddr=\[?(?:::ffff:)?([0-9a-f:.]*?[0-9a-f])\]?:\d+(?=\s|$)))</regex>
  <order>srcip, adguard.ip</order>
</decoder>

<decoder name="adguard-home-log-fields">
  <parent>adguard-home-log</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">(?:url=|uri=| )(/control/[^\s:?"]+)</regex>
  <order>url</order>
</decoder>

<decoder name="adguard-home-log-fields">
  <parent>adguard-home-log</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2"> user(?|=([^\s"]+)|="([^"]*)"| "([^"]*)")</regex>
  <order>dstuser</order>
</decoder>

<decoder name="adguard-home-log-fields">
  <parent>adguard-home-log</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2"> status=(\d+)</regex>
  <order>adguard.status</order>
</decoder>

<decoder name="adguard-home-log-fields">
  <parent>adguard-home-log</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2"> path \\?"([^"\\]+)\\?" does not match safe patterns</regex>
  <order>adguard.path</order>
</decoder>

<decoder name="adguard-home-log-fields">
  <parent>adguard-home-log</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">AdGuard Home, version (v?\d[^\s",]*)</regex>
  <order>adguard.version</order>
</decoder>

<decoder name="adguard-home-log-fields">
  <parent>adguard-home-log</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">deleted filter id=(-?\d+)</regex>
  <order>adguard.filter_id</order>
</decoder>

<decoder name="adguard-home-log-fields">
  <parent>adguard-home-log</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">pprof: listening addr=(\S+)</regex>
  <order>adguard.addr</order>
</decoder>
